#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# circle

#% text
# `circle([x, y], r)` is a graphics primitive: the circle of radius r centred at [x, y].

#% text
# `circle([x, y], [rx, ry])` is an ellipse with semi-axes rx and ry along the coordinate axes.

#% text
# `circle([x, y], r, [a0, a1])` is the arc from angle a0 to angle a1, in radians, anticlockwise.

#% text
# `circle()` is the unit circle at the origin.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The circle is an outline drawn in the current colour, `thickness` and `dashing`; `disk` is the filled shape.

#% item
# Radii are in plot coordinates.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A unit circle:

#% cell
graphics(circle())
#% output
#= image("circle.files/circle-1.svgz")

#% text
# Concentric circles in two colours:

#% cell
graphics([blue, circle([0, 0], 1), red, circle([0, 0], 2)])
#% output
#= image("circle.files/circle-2.svgz")

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# An arc and an ellipse:

#% cell
graphics([circle([0, 0], 1, [0, pi]), circle([0, -1], [2, 0.5])])
#% output
#= image("circle.files/circle-3.svgz")

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# The angles of an arc are a list of two:

#% cell
circle([0, 0], 1, 3)
#% output
#! failure(argument, circle, 3)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `disk`, `bezier_curve`, `thickness`, `graphics`
